{
  "raw": {
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9721/tenure-track%2c-assistant-professor---graduate-school-of-professional-psychology/job": {
      "title": "Tenure-Track, Assistant Professor - Graduate School of Professional Psychology",
      "description": "Tenure-Track, Assistant Professor - Graduate School of Professional Psychology Posted Date 1 week ago (10/5/2026 1:20 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW POSITION DESCRIPTION The Graduate School of Professional Psychology (GSPP) at the University of St. Thomas invites applications for a tenure-track faculty member. The position is a full-time, 9-month position at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning September 1, 2027. The GSPP welcomes candidates who are committed to the mission of the college, which centers on health equity and social justice, being in solidarity with underrepresented and marginalized populations, and creating a culture of belonging. We are particularly interested in applicants with professional experience in assessment and intervention across the lifespan. We welcome applications from candidates from a variety of specialties, particularly when their expertise complements existing curriculum and creates new opportunities for students. Teaching responsibilities may vary depending upon the candidate’s backgrounds and interests, and could include coursework in any or all of the following areas: Introductory, Advanced, or Developmental Psychopathology, Lifespan Development, Child & Adolescent Assessment and Intervention, Measurement, Personality and Cognitive Assessment, Trauma and Counseling, Health Psychology, and Geropsychology. Part-time consultation and clinical practice are encouraged, consistent with our practitioner-scholar model. Previous experience with accreditation processes, training, and other administrative work is also valued. The GSPP is housed within the interdisciplinary Morrison Family College of Health. The GSPP aims to train practitioner-scholars who integrate the science of psychology with self-reflective practice; think systemically and developmentally; and provide ethical, inclusive, and culturally sensitive services to advance the common good. The GSPP community is committed to providing a supportive learning environment that actively seeks and values diversity among its students, staff, and faculty. This includes diversity in cultures, values, and experiences; diversity in perspectives including theoretical models, “ways of knowing” and associated scholarly paradigms; and diversity in ways of healing and engaging in professional practice. Faculty, staff, and students in the GSPP strive to uphold a collegial and supportive culture. Responsibilities Teach and advise MA and PsyD students Supervise doctoral dissertations Engage in scholarship and service at the school, university, and community levels. INSTITUTIONAL PROFILE The mission of the Morrison Family College of Health (MFCOH) is to advance whole person health by preparing highly skilled and caring professionals who are culturally responsive, practice with ingenuity, and proactively advance health equity and social justice. In it are the academic units of the Graduate School of Professional Psychology, Department of Health and Exercise Science, Susan S. Morrison School of Nursing, and the School of Social Work. The Morrison Family College of Health’s Vision, Mission, and Guiding Principles can be found here. The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission driven, urban university with over 9,000 students and 400 full-time faculty. It is Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. In 2019, the Morrison Family College of Health became the newest addition to the institution’s eight different schools and colleges. The GSPP is located in University of St. Thomas’ vibrant downtown Minneapolis campus, one of the nation’s top 25 metro areas. U.S. News ranked Minneapolis and St. Paul as one of the most racially diverse U.S. cities. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. SALARY AND BENEFITS The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary Range: $75,000 to $90,000 The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Required qualifications: Have earned a doctoral degree (PsyD, PhD) (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) from an APA-accredited Counseling Psychology, Clinical Psychology, or School Psychology program or from a related discipline; alternatively, ABD will be considered. A completed doctorate is preferred, but applicants who will complete their degree by September 2027 will be considered We are particularly interested in candidates committed to teaching a student body with varied backgrounds, perspectives, and life experiences. Commitment to mentor, advise, and teach graduate students in both a practitioner-oriented MA Program and an APA-accredited practitioner-scholar doctoral program in Counseling Psychology. Be willing to attend on-campus meetings and events on a regular basis. How to Apply: To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/. The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) in which the applicant will summarize their education, skills, teaching, and professional experience Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific cover letter detailing Interest in the opportunity Teaching and/or training philosophy and experience Professional practice and scholarly interests Past and present efforts to promote diversity, equity, inclusion , and belonging (DEIB) and, future aspirations in support of DEIB Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process All application materials must be submitted online through the University of St. Thomas employment site. To ensure full consideration for the position, review will begin on October 29, 2026; however, the position will remain open until filled. Questions about the position should be directed to the search committee chair, Dr. Salina Renninger (renn8979@stthomas.edu). If there are any issues with the application process, please submit an AskHR ticket here . Established in 1885, the University of St. Thomas is located in the major metropolitan area of Minneapolis-St. Paul, and is Minnesota’s largest private university. Its 10,000 students pursue degrees in a wide range of liberal arts, professional, and graduate programs. Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9712/tenure-track-in-social-work-with-clinical-focus/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track in Social Work with Clinical Focus",
      "description": "Tenure Track in Social Work with Clinical Focus Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/1/2026 10:14 AM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The School of Social Work at the University of St. Thomas invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Social Work position with a nine-month appointment. We seek a dynamic and engaged scholar-educator who demonstrates collaborative engagement, excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service. This candidate will contribute meaningfully to our commitment of preparing ethical, competent, and culturally responsive social work professionals. Our School offers social work degree programs at the baccalaureate, master's, and doctoral levels. This in-person position will have primary teaching responsibilities in the clinical MSW program and may include teaching at the BSW or DSW level as needed. The successful candidate will have demonstrated training, skills, and experience to teach clinical courses. The MSW program is offered in two formats: in-person weeknights and hybrid weekends. The BSW program is offered in the traditional weekday format, and the DSW is fully online. Primary Responsibilities: Teach six social work courses per year Teach across multiple formats (in-person weeknights and hybrid weekends) Student advising and mentoring Engage in scholarly work, research, publications, and community impact Contribute to curriculum development, innovation, assessment, and CSWE accreditation processes Participate in departmental, college, university, and community service ABOUT THE SCHOOL AND COLLEGE The School of Social Work mission is: Grounded in social justice and inspired by Catholic social thought, we educate and engage students to learn, grow and develop their professional skills as they prepare for social work practice in support of health, wellbeing and the common good. We recently celebrated its 50th anniversary as an accredited school. Our faculty and staff are committed to helping students develop a strong clinical social work identity, grounded in social work values and ethics; the skills to actively engage diverse client populations; a strong knowledge base with critical thinking and reflective practice skills; the capacity for leadership; and the capacity to assess and improve social work practice. Our MSW program has special expertise and programming in integrated behavioral health, practice with older adults, practice with immigrants and refugees, and addiction and co-occurring disorders. The School of Social Work is part of the Morrison Family College of Health. We invite candidates who will work to advance the missions of both the School and the College, the latter being, to prepare highly skilled and caring professionals who are culturally responsive, practice with ingenuity, and proactively advance health equity and social justice. ABOUT THE UNIVERSITY OF ST. THOMAS The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. To learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas, please see https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/for-faculty/index.html . The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. SALARY AND BENEFITS The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary range for this position is $68,000 - $80,000 The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications: MSW from a CSWE-accredited program and at least two years of post-MSW clinical social work practice experience PhD or DSW (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) in Social Work or a closely related field (ABD considered if all degree requirements are completed by August 1, 2027) Ability and strong interest in teaching clinical social work courses at the graduate level Experience in teaching, and interest in teaching in both in-person and hybrid learning environments Commitment to the values and ethics of social work and the mission and convictions of the university Minnesota state law requires that individuals with social work degrees who teach must be licensed. Candidates from Minnesota must hold a clinical license (LICSW). Those outside of MN must hold a clinical license in localities that have this option, and if not, they must be clinical license-eligible. Preferred Qualifications: Clinical expertise in one or more of the following areas: behavioral health, integrated healthcare, substance use and addictions, clinical practice across the lifespan and with underserved populations, trauma-informed practice, clinical assessment, or implementation of evidence-based interventions Experience with interprofessional collaboration, and/or community engaged practice Experience with curriculum development and pedagogical innovation Experience securing and/or administering external funding (e.g., grants, contracts) HOW TO APPLY To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Social work license number, licensure state, and type; or indicate if you are not currently licensed but are license-eligible. Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific cover letter that expresses why you are applying for this position, your teaching philosophy, and your program of scholarship. The letter should outline relevant experience and how that experience has prepared you for the role, including evidence of successful teaching Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process All application materials must be submitted online through the University of St. Thomas employment site. Questions regarding the position should be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Ankita Deka, at deka4813@stthomas.edu. Application review begins October 30, 2026. To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. The position will remain open until filled. Several faculty members from the School of Social Work will be attending CSWE 2026 in Atlanta, and our school will also be hosting a booth! We would love to connect with you. Please don’t hesitate to stop by our booth, say hello, and learn more about our programs. In light of its commitment to create and maintain a safe learning and working environment, employment with the University of St. Thomas requires consent to and successful completion of a background screening. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9718/teaching-professor%2c-theology--dougherty-family-college/job": {
      "title": "Teaching Professor, Theology- Dougherty Family College",
      "description": "Teaching Professor, Theology- Dougherty Family College Posted Date 2 weeks ago (10/1/2026 10:21 AM) Campus Minneapolis Employment Type Teaching Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW THE OPPORTUNITY The University of St. Thomas invites applications for a full-time, 10-month Teaching Professor position in Theology for the university’s two-year college, the Dougherty Family College. This is a non-tenure-track, renewable position. The faculty member will teach an in-person, 100-level Theology course (e.g., Foundations of the Christian Tradition) and serve as academic advisor and mentor to a cohort of students. We seek colleagues with a record of success in teaching theology courses, expertise in culturally responsive pedagogy, and a commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion. Faculty are expected to teach in-person three sections of theology per semester and provide intensive mentoring and advising. The successful candidate will also teach an online course during January and every other summer (additional compensation is given for online courses). This faculty member will also provide service to the college and university (support committee work, etc.). This is a full-time 10-month non-tenure track, renewable position (starting August 1, 2027). THE UNIVERSITY Established in 1885, the University of St. Thomas is located in Minneapolis-St. Paul, and is Minnesota’s largest private university. Its 10,000 students pursue degrees in a wide range of liberal arts, professional, and graduate programs. As a community, the University of St. Thomas is committed to: Pursuit of truth - We value intellectual inquiry as a life-long habit, the unfettered and impartial pursuit of truth in all its forms, the integration of knowledge across disciplines, and the imaginative and creative exploration of new ideas. Academic excellence - We create a culture among faculty, students, and staff that recognizes the power of ideas and rewards rigorous thinking. Faith and reason - We actively engage Catholic intellectual tradition, which values the fundamental compatibility of faith and reason and fosters meaningful dialogue directed toward the flourishing of human culture. Dignity - We respect the dignity of each person and value the unique contributions that each brings to the greater mosaic of the university community. Diversity - We strive to create a vibrant, diverse community in which, together, we work for a more just and inclusive society. Personal attention - We foster a caring culture that supports the well-being of each member. Gratitude - We celebrate the achievements of all members of our community in goals attained and obstacles overcome, and in all things give praise to God. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity, and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range is for this position is $58,000- $63,000 a year (depending on education and experience) plus health benefits. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS PROFESSIONAL EXPERIENCE/QUALIFICATIONS/EDUCATION We seek candidates who recognize the value of high expectations, student-teacher relationships, and home-school-community connections for students’ academic achievement. Competitive candidates will demonstrate how their teaching record fosters successful learning communities reflective of their students’ diverse cultures. Candidates will also demonstrate how their teaching has prompted students to make use of their culturally embedded knowledge, resulting in meaningful learning that affirms their students’ social identities and connections while promoting high academic performance. Successful candidates will have a background in critical and/or culturally responsive pedagogies and recognize their own cultural experiences, identities, manifestation of privilege, and the dynamics these evoke in and outside of the classroom. Candidates will embrace self-reflection, value excellence through educational equity, and demonstrate a commitment to ongoing culturally responsive learning in a collaborative, collegial environment. We are seeking highly motivated, innovative, and creative educators who will provide intensive mentoring and advising to students. Faculty mentors are expected to help students earn their associate of arts degrees and prepare them with the solid educational foundation to successfully complete their bachelor’s degrees. Faculty are expected to be on campus and available to students at least four days a week 8:30 am – 4:30 pm. The academic class schedule is very structured. The faculty member will teach three sections of Theology in the Fall semester and three every Spring, with an additional January online course and a summer course (also online) every other year. The three theology sections are each scheduled for a 2-hour block twice a week (total of 4 hours of instructional time per section each week). Faculty will meet with each advisee (approximately twenty-five advisees) for individual mentoring sessions at minimum 20 minutes a month. Faculty will meet with their entire cohort in Leadership Advisory Groups (LAG) at minimum 1 hour a month. In addition to teaching, mentoring, and service to the college/university, faculty are expected to work with students during the August summer enrichment program and perform other responsibilities related to college program development and assessment. Required: master’s degree (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) in theology or a closely related discipline. Candidates with at least one year of college teaching experience (community college teaching experience a plus) are strongly encouraged to apply. How to Apply: To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/. The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) a one-page statement that presents the candidate’s philosophy of developing and leading initiatives to support a culturally responsive curriculum (discuss culturally responsive pedagogy in the statement) and evidence of teaching excellence with underserved students (e.g., student feedback on your teaching) Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific cover letter detailing your interest in the position Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process Application Deadline: January 15, 2027 All application materials must be submitted online through the University of St. Thomas employment site. Established in 1885, the University of St. Thomas is located in the major metropolitan area of Minneapolis-St. Paul, and is Minnesota’s largest private university. Its 10,000 students pursue degrees in a wide range of liberal arts, professional, and graduate programs. Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9678/dsw-program-director%2c-associate-tenure-track-faculty/job": {
      "title": "DSW Program Director, Associate Tenure-Track Faculty",
      "description": "DSW Program Director, Associate Tenure-Track Faculty Posted Date 3 weeks ago (9/23/2026 4:07 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The University of St. Thomas, Minnesota’s largest private university, seeks an Associate tenure-track faculty member who will serve as the Doctor in Social Work (DSW) Program Director for the School of Social Work (SSW). Position Summary The University of St. Thomas invites qualified candidates to apply for the DSW Program Director-Tenure Track Faculty Member position, a 9-month faculty appointment with administrative compensation that extends to 12 months. This position will begin in August of 2027. Candidates will build on the SSW’s strengths and lead the DSW Program into the future. They will support the success of doctoral students, faculty, and staff through their leadership and guidance. They will have a track record of excellence in teaching and scholarship, and success in managing social work programming. The qualified candidate will be expected to teach courses, advise doctoral students, and lead program accreditation and curriculum development efforts. Grounded in social justice and inspired by Catholic social thought, our mission is to educate and engage students to learn, grow, and develop their professional skills as they prepare for social work practice in support of health, well-being, and the common good. As part of the Morrison Family College of Health (MFCOH), the SSW collaborates with the School of Nursing, the Graduate School of Professional Psychology, and the Department of Health and Exercise Science to prepare highly skilled and caring professionals who are culturally responsive, practice with ingenuity, and proactively advance health equity and social justice. The University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a vibrant, mission-driven, urban university. The main campus is located in a historic St. Paul neighborhood with easy access to downtown St. Paul and Minneapolis. In addition to being Minnesota’s largest private university, we are one of the largest Catholic colleges or universities in the nation, with more than 9,000 undergraduate and graduate students at our eight schools and colleges . Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas. The DSW Program at the University of St. Thomas For more than a decade, the St. Thomas DSW Program has prepared social workers across the country to lead, educate, and create meaningful change. As the first fully online DSW program of its kind, it now offers two areas of emphasis: one in higher education , and the other in transformational leadership in the community and public–private sectors. The DSW program is part of the St. Thomas SSW, a nationally respected School that has been shaping social work education and practice for more than 50 years. Designed for experienced social workers with an MSW, the program combines academic excellence with flexibility for working professionals. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity, and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Key Responsibilities Teaching and Scholarship Teaches graduate doctoral courses and advises doctoral students within the school (reduced teaching load). Maintains active professional engagement or scholarly research agenda in their area of expertise. Curricular and Programmatic Leadership Innovates curriculum development and revision processes. Oversees DSW accreditation and program assessment processes and reporting. Generates course and faculty scheduling for the program in consultation with the SSW Chair. Guides directed and independent study proposals for the program as needed. Facilitates transfer credit decisions and updates the transfer credit tool. Day-to-Day Operations Develops the Academic Program budget and provides planning, oversight, and forecasting of the DSW budget. Oversees the program budget and provides regular updates to the Chair. Assures that social work policies, operating frameworks, curriculum, and practices align with university and college-level decisions and those of external regulatory entities. Responsible for DSW program academic infrastructure and shared governance activities to ensure optimal functioning and ethical generation and use of resources in support of the University’s mission. Addresses student academic concerns. Supports recruiting and marketing efforts, conducts informational sessions, and meets with prospective students. Supports faculty, staff, and adjunct faculty hiring processes and onboarding for the program as requested by the SSW Chair. Contributes to annual evaluations of adjunct faculty and to the academic unit reporting as requested by the SSW Chair. Strategic Communications and Stakeholder Management. Serves as a liaison between program faculty/staff and Chair/Dean’s Office. Represents the academic program, SSW, and MFCOH both in and outside of the university. Provides thought leadership about social work and is the primary spokesperson for the SSW’s DSW Program at St. Thomas. Participates on the MFCOH Leadership Team, and key standing committees. Communicates openly and transparently with both internal and external stakeholders. Cultivates and stewards new and existing partnerships between the SSW and external entities. Initiates, writes, and reviews materials, reports, and communications. Actively engages in advocacy for the DSW Program. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range is for this position is $80,000 - $100,000 with a 9-month contract, depending on determined rank. Additional administrative compensation for the DSW Program Director role is between $6200-$18,000 and depends on the scope of responsibility throughout a 12-month period. One term as DSW Program Director is 4 years, with this appointment renewable upon mutual agreement with formal consultation from the faculty. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications A PhD (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) or DSW in social work, with a minimum of two years post-MSW practice experience. Record of teaching and scholarly achievements. Possibility for expedited tenure review upon hiring. Administrative or management experience in an academic institution of a comparable profile. Master’s degree in social work from a CSWE accredited institution. Licensed in the State of Minnesota (or license-eligible and gaining such licensure within a year of residency). Understand CSWE accreditation standards and process, including curriculum alignment and compliance. Demonstrated experience with student recruitment and retention. Ability to guide academic and curricular development responsive to community needs and professional trends. Experience with shared governance to support the institutional mission. Familiarity with budget oversight and planning. Strong communication skills. Preferred Qualifications Minimum of three years of administrative or management experience in an academic institution of a comparable profile. Rank of associate professor, with scholarly work that could be counted toward the rank of full professor. Record of external funding, including successful grant writing and administration. Track record of serving on college or university leadership teams. Experience leading through change in higher education. How to Apply: To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Written statements discussing their teaching philosophy, area of research and scholarship, philosophy of leadership, and commitment to the mission of the MFCOH and the University. Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific cover letter detailing your interest in the position. Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms. Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission. Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process. All application materials must be submitted online through the University of St. Thomas employment site. Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9637/adjunct-in-developmental-psychology/job": {
      "title": "Adjunct in Developmental Psychology",
      "description": "Adjunct in Developmental Psychology Posted Date 1 month ago (9/8/2026 10:06 AM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Adjunct Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The University of St. Thomas Psychology Department invites applications for an adjunct faculty appointment to teach PSYC 202: Lifespan Development , a foundational undergraduate course examining prenatal development through late adulthood, including physical growth, brain development, cognitive changes, emotional and social development, and research designs. The department anticipates a need for one to two sections for Spring 2027 , with additional teaching opportunities possible in future semesters based on department needs and enrollments. We are particularly interested in candidates with academic training and/or teaching experience in developmental psychology, human development, lifespan development, or a closely related area . Candidates with experience teaching lifespan or developmental psychology at the undergraduate level are especially encouraged to apply. All courses are taught in-person on the St. Paul campus of the University of St. Thomas. The salary range for the College of Arts and Sciences Adjunct positions ranges from $5,994 to $6,717 (per 4 credit undergraduate course, 3 credit graduate course). Rate of pay is determined by academic credential and years of teaching experience. Teaching load generally ranges from one to two lectures sections per semester, depending upon department needs and enrollments. We seek candidates interested in facilitating meaningful experiences between students and faculty that empower undergraduate students to find success and to solve challenging problems in service of the common good. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. QUALIFICATIONS Qualifications We will consider candidates with an advanced degree (master's or doctorate) in Psychology, Developmental Psychology, Human Development, or a closely related field. ABD doctoral candidates are encouraged to apply. Preferred qualifications: Graduate training in developmental psychology, human development, psychology, or a closely related field; Experience teaching lifespan development, developmental psychology, or related undergraduate courses; Familiarity with contemporary developmental research, theory, and evidence-based approaches to understanding human development across the lifespan; Ability to engage undergraduate students with the major concepts, theories, and empirical findings in developmental psychology; Interest in fostering meaningful relationships with and mentoring undergraduate students; Commitment to inclusive and student-centered teaching practices. HOW TO APPLY Candidates will be asked to copy/paste a cover letter into the application and submit a curriculum vitae with professional references. The cover letter should include which disciplines the candidate is interested in teaching. The curriculum vitae should provide evidence that the candidate is qualified to teach the preferred discipline. If the candidate wishes to be considered for a lecture position, they should also include evidence of teaching effectiveness as part of the application. No references will be contacted without first getting the permission of the candidate. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9624/tenure-track%2c-assistant-or-associate%2c-mechanical-engineering/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track, Assistant or Associate, Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure Track, Assistant or Associate, Mechanical Engineering Posted Date 1 month ago (9/3/2026 5:24 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Mechanical Engineering Department in the College of Engineering at the University of St. Thomas is seeking applications for a tenure track faculty position. This is an exciting time for growth in the Mechanical Engineering program at St Thomas, as we have recently moved into the new $110M Schoenecker Center with state-of-the-art engineering labs, including a prototyping center, robotics and automation lab, and an advanced materials lab, as well as an expansion of our Manufacturing & Machining Center. As a student-focused institution with a strong hands-on curriculum, we emphasize undergraduate and graduate education that provides technical excellence with a significant emphasis on real world applications. Our undergraduate curriculum includes minors in Aerospace Engineering and Biomedical Engineering, providing students with opportunities to explore these applications. Successful candidates will be expected to develop and deliver engaging undergraduate and graduate courses and to establish and maintain an active research program. Candidates should be highly qualified to teach undergraduate dynamics, including topics in particle and rigid-body kinematics and kinetics. We welcome applicants from a broad range of areas that build upon or complement this foundation, including, but not limited to, multibody and three-dimensional rigid-body dynamics, robotics and robotic mechanisms, flight dynamics, aerospace engineering, biomedical engineering and other areas relevant to mechanical engineering. Faculty at UST have the freedom to grow and pursue excellence in their area of engineering passion as educators and researchers. The University’s location in the thriving community of Minneapolis-St. Paul provides a vibrant platform for projects in a broad range of high-tech disciplines. Join our growing team as we work with the next generation of engineers in an environment where interdisciplinary work, discovery and innovation is a way of life. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas . The College of Engineering actively seeks to attract, retain and develop faculty, staff, and students from all backgrounds to enhance the diversity of the learning community, and to offer our students richly varied perspectives. The University of St. Thomas embraces diversity, inclusion, and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion a diverse, equitable and inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. We are committed to building a skilled team that represents a variety of backgrounds and perspectives. This commitment is consistent with our mission statement: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will have a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Salary & Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary range for this position is $77,500 - $116,000 The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD (U.S. or Foreign Equivalent) in a relevant Engineering discipline is required, as well as a clearly articulated vision for advancing a research program. For candidates who are nearing the completion of their PhD, a timeline for Ph.D. completion will be discussed during the interview process and agreed upon prior to extension of an offer. All applicants must have demonstrable skills necessary for excellence in teaching. For candidates whose primary experience is in the engineering industry (outside of academia), skill in teaching may be demonstrated based on other professional experiences which require leadership, mentorship and presentation skills. Review of applications will begin on 11/1/26 and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact the search committee chair, Dr. Katherine Acton (kacton@stthomas.edu) if you have any questions. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: a curriculum vitae a written statement of teaching philosophy, which highlights Mechanical Engineering courses the candidate would be interested in teaching. a written statement of research interests, and a plan to develop a successful research program at St. Thomas. A successful research program at St. Thomas is one that would support student research and advance the national profile of the College of Engineering Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that highlights the applicant’s interest in our program and describes a specific experience that illustrates how they have put one or more of the institution’s ideals into practice. (The ideals of the institution are outlined above in the institutional mission statement. ) Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three-five professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three-five letters of recommendation Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9627/tenure-track-assistant-professor%2c-electrical-%26-computer-engineering/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Electrical & Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Electrical & Computer Engineering Posted Date 1 month ago (9/3/2026 5:31 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Electrical & Computer Engineering in the College of Engineering at the University of Saint Thomas, Saint Paul, MN is seeking applications for a tenure track assistant professor position to start in Fall 2027. This is an exciting time for growth in the Electrical & Computer Engineering program at St Thomas, as we have recently expanded into the new $110M Schoenecker Center with state-of-the-art engineering labs. The Electrical & Computer Engineering Department offers undergraduate degrees in Electrical Engineering and Computer Engineering and a master’s degree in Electrical & Computer Engineering. As a student-focused, liberal arts institution with a strong hands-on curriculum, we emphasize undergraduate and graduate education that provides technical excellence with a significant focus on real world applications. Successful candidates will be expected to teach foundational undergraduate courses, develop and deliver an engaging set of courses in their area of expertise, as well as establish and maintain an active research program. Applications from candidates with specialization in any area of Electrical & Computer Engineering are welcome. Relevant Industry experience is highly valued. About the University of St. Thomas Faculty at the University of St. Thomas have the freedom to grow and pursue excellence in their area of engineering passion as educators and researchers. The University’s location in the thriving community of Minneapolis-St. Paul provides a vibrant platform for projects in a broad range of high-tech disciplines. Join our growing team as we work with the next generation of engineers in an environment where interdisciplinary work, discovery and innovation is a way of life. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas . The College of Engineering actively seeks to attract, retain and develop faculty, staff, and students from all backgrounds to enhance the diversity of the learning community, and to offer our students richly varied perspectives. The University of St. Thomas embraces diversity, inclusion, and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion a diverse, equitable and inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. We are committed to building a skilled team that represents a variety of backgrounds and perspectives. This commitment is consistent with our mission statement: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will have a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary range for this position is $77,500 - $116,000 The University of Saint Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the university may pay for position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD degree (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related field is required, as well as a clearly articulated vision for advancing a research program. For candidates who are nearing the completion of their PhD, a timeline for completion will be discussed during the interview process and agreed upon prior to the extension of an offer. Review of applications will begin o n 10/26/26 and will continue until the position is filled. Please contact the search committee chair, Dr. Hassan Salamy ( hsalamy@stthomas.edu ) if you have any questions. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: a curriculum vitae A written statement of teaching philosophy, which highlights Electrical & Computer Engineering courses the candidate would be interested in teaching. A written statement of research interests, and a plan to develop a successful research program at St. Thomas. A successful research program at St. Thomas is one that would support student research and advance the national profile of the College of Engineering. Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that highlights the applicant’s interest in our program and commitment to the ideals of the institution. The ideals of the institution are outlined above in the institutional mission statement. Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three-five professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three-five letters of recommendation Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
