    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9635/tenure-track-assistant-professor%2c-public-health/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Public Health",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Public Health Posted Date 1 month ago (9/3/2026 5:18 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Health and Exercise Science in the Morrison Family College of Health at the University of St. Thomas in St. Paul, MN invites applicants for a tenure-track assistant professor faculty position in public health. We seek applicants who are committed to excellence in teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels. A successful applicant will be interested and able to teach a range of public health undergraduate and graduate courses that may include epidemiology and computational analysis, healthcare systems, health policy, one health, and environmental and occupational health. In addition, they will provide academic advising to students and supervise internship and applied practice projects. A successful applicant will have experience in conducting and disseminating peer reviewed research, with the potential to develop a robust scholarly agenda, and the desire to provide service to the department, college, university, and greater community. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university with 10,000 students and 450 full-time faculty. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to both downtown Minneapolis and St. Paul, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with 150+ undergraduate majors/minors and 55 graduate programs. In 2019, the Morrison Family College of Health became the newest addition to the institution’s eight different schools and colleges. The college is comprised of several departments and schools, including the Department of Health and Exercise Science (HES), that was established prior to the college’s inception and houses the undergraduate public health major and the Master of Public Health online program, launching Fall 2026. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas. About the Health and Exercise Science Department The interdisciplinary Department of HES engages in innovative teaching, research, service, and community-based initiatives intended to promote both personal and population health. Health equity or the idea that everyone should have the opportunity to be healthy is central to all of our work. As such, HES faculty and staff strive to create inclusive learning spaces that embrace the diverse backgrounds, perspectives, and worldviews of our students, faculty, staff, and the communities we serve. We also commit to ensuring equitable access to our programs such that we might train a workforce that reflects the rich diversity of our collective community and is committed to health equity. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary range is for this position is $64,000-$74,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Qualified candidates for the position must have the following: A terminal degree (Ph.D., Dr.P.H., or equivalent), U.S. or Foreign Equivalent, in public health, epidemiology, global health, health policy, or related health field. Alternatively, ABD candidates nearing completion of their PH.D. will be considered. Evidence of teaching effectiveness from prior teaching experience at the undergraduate and/or graduate level. At least one year of teaching experience required, which may include experience teaching on record as a teaching assistant. Experience teaching different course modalities (in-person, online, blended, experiential). At least one year of experience in conducting and disseminating peer reviewed research, with the potential to develop a robust research agenda. At least one year of current or recent public health practical experience. Interest and/or experience working collaboratively (interdisciplinary). How to Apply To ensure full consideration for the position, all application materials should be received by September 30, 2026 ; however, the position will remain open until filled. Questions about the position should be directed to the search committee chair (Dr. Amy Hedman-Robertson, amy.robertson@stthomas.edu). To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/. The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae: Listing education, teaching, research and public health practical experience. Statement of Teaching Philosophy, Effectiveness, and Inclusive Pedagogy: Describe your teaching philosophy, summarize your pedagogical approach, and provide evidence of teaching effectiveness, including examples of student learning outcomes, instructional innovation, assessment practices, and strategies used to create an inclusive and supportive learning environment. Expected length: 500-700 words. Statement of Research Agenda: Briefly summarize your current and future research agenda, including your primary areas of inquiry, scholarly goals, and the anticipated impact of your work on your discipline, profession, or community. Expected length: 500-700 words. Although the search committee may request further information from candidates as the search progresses, please do not upload any additional documents into the application platform at this time. Official transcripts will be requested at a later date, but no additional materials beyond those requested above are asked of you at this time. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a unique job-specific cover letter detailing your interest in the position Complete the required voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self-identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide a list of names, position titles, and contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process Finalists may be required to produce letters of reference All application materials must be submitted online through the University of St. Thomas employment site. Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9619/tenure-track-faculty%2c-philosophy/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Faculty, Philosophy",
      "description": "Tenure Track Faculty, Philosophy Posted Date 1 month ago (9/1/2026 11:03 AM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Philosophy Department in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas (St. Paul, Minnesota) invites applications for a tenure-track position in the area of applied ethics to begin September 1, 2027. Subfields of particular interest include the ethics of artificial intelligence, data, and digital technology. Candidates specializing in other areas of applied ethics are also encouraged to apply, as are candidates whose specialization relates AI, computing, or technology more broadly to metaphysics, epistemology, phenomenology, philosophy of language, philosophy of science, or philosophy of mind. The teaching load is six, four-credit courses per year (semester system). Ideal candidates will possess outstanding reasoning skills, excellence in teaching philosophy at the undergraduate level with the ability to connect with students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests, strong research potential, a commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration, and will provide service to the department and/or university. In addition to teaching courses in their area of specialization, qualified candidates should be well prepared to teach our department’s introductory course, The Person and the Good. About the Department of Philosophy The University of St. Thomas is home to a thriving undergraduate philosophy program—one that graduates 30 to 40 majors each year along with an equally large number of minors. Our department is committed to sustaining and developing the Catholic intellectual tradition and we seek candidates who will both (i) support our university’s institutional mission and its emphasis on Catholic intellectual tradition and (ii) expand our department’s interdisciplinary outreach. More information about the College of Arts & Sciences and the Department of Philosophy can be found at https://www.stthomas.edu/artsandsciences/ . More information on the Philosophy curriculum can be found at https://www.stthomas.edu/catalog/curricula/philosophy/index.html. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan areas and Minneapolis-St. Paul international airport, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas at the following link: https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/for-faculty/index.html. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website: https://www.stthomas.edu/mission/ . Salary & Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range for this position is $70,000-$75,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Applicants should have a PhD (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) in philosophy or a closely related discipline, or alternatively, ABD nearing completion will also be considered. Applicants must be able to demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and show strong potential for productive research/scholarly activities. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: A Curriculum Vitae (CV) A teaching portfolio that includes a statement of teaching philosophy, a sample syllabus, and two pieces of evidence demonstrating teaching effectiveness (which may include student reports on instruction, peer teaching evaluations , other types of teaching assessment, and/or statement of approach for successful teaching in philosophy courses). Please include all elements of the teaching portfolio in a single (.docx or .pdf) document. A research statement A scholarly writing sample Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job specific cover letter detailing your qualifications as they pertain to the overview of the advertised position and your approach to engagement with the university’s mission and convictions Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self-identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Reference checks typically occur during the later stages of the search process Questions regarding the position should be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Jonathan Stoltz at jestoltz@stthomas.edu. Application review begins October 29, 2026 . To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. The position will remain open until filled. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9579/tenure-track-assistant-professor%2c-biology/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Biology",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Biology Posted Date 2 months ago (8/25/2026 2:00 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Biology Department in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas (St. Paul, Minnesota) invites applications for a tenure-track position in Genetics, Ecology, and Evolution . The position will be at the rank of Assistant Professor to begin in September 2027. Candidates who integrate genetics and ecology (for example, population genetics, conservation genetics, or molecular ecology) are particularly encouraged to apply. We seek candidates who are committed to exemplifying the teacher-scholar model. The successful candidate will demonstrate potential to teach undergraduate biology courses at the introductory and upper-division levels, with options including BIOL 207 Genetics, Ecology, and Evolution; BIOL 209 Biology of Sustainability; a 300-level course in the candidate’s area of expertise; and a 400-level senior capstone course that integrates the candidate’s research. We seek candidates with a commitment to using inclusive classroom practices that support students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests. The teaching load is six, four-credit courses per year; two of these six courses per year are reallocated for the expected mentorship of undergraduate research collaborators. The successful candidate will demonstrate potential to establish a vibrant, high-quality research program that has the potential to be competitive for external funding and that will prominently involve undergraduates of all experience levels and from varied backgrounds. Research support will include start-up funds and laboratory/computing space in a science facility. The successful candidate will also advise undergraduate biology majors and provide service to the department, College of Arts & Sciences, and/or the university. About the Biology Department The Biology Department is a professionally engaged community that equips undergraduate students to find their passions, embark on meaningful careers, and become responsible, scientifically literate citizens through authentic experiences in science. We are home to approximately 80 student majors each year and offer B.S. and B.A. degrees in Biology, as well as a B.S in Biology of Global Health. We also support interdisciplinary undergraduate programs in Biochemistry, Neuroscience, Environmental Science, and Data Analytics. More information on the Biology curriculum, including a list of our courses, can be found here . Our research facilities are well equipped with state-of-the-art instrumentation, and our full-time faculty are recipients of major, externally funded research grants. Research collaborations with undergraduates routinely result in student presentations at national conferences and student authorship on peer-reviewed publications. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. Learn more about life as a faculty member at the Join the Faculty at University of St. Thomas website. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. The successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range is for this position is $68,000 - $75,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications A PhD (U.S. or foreign equivalent) in Biology or related field, or alternatively, ABD candidates nearing completion of the PhD will also be considered, with an initial appointment made at the rank of instructor. Applicants must be able to demonstrate research expertise in Genetics, Ecology, and Evolution. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: A curriculum vitae (CV) A 2-page statement of teaching philosophy that includes a discussion of introductory and upper-level courses the applicant would be interested in teaching in our current curriculum, upper-level courses that would enhance our current offerings, and the potential for inclusive classroom practices to support students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests A 2-page statement of short- and long-term research goals that includes a discussion of establishing a vibrant, high-quality research program that has the potential to be competitive for external funding, and how the applicant will involve and mentor undergraduate collaborators in their research Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that describes the applicant’s interest in the position, and relates the applicant’s professional qualifications to the responsibilities of the position Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation Questions regarding the position should be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Carolyn Zeiner ( carolyn.zeiner@stthomas.edu ). Review of applications will begin on October 15, 2026 . To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. The position will remain open until filled. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9578/tenure-track-assistant-professor%2c-earth%2c-environment%2c-and-society/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Earth, Environment, and Society",
      "description": "Tenure Track Assistant Professor, Earth, Environment, and Society Posted Date 2 months ago (8/24/2026 11:37 AM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Earth, Environment, and Society in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Environmental Science, Environmental Studies, Geography, Geographic Information Science (GIS), or a closely related field, beginning Fall 2027. We seek an interdisciplinary environmental scholar who applies geospatial analysis — including Geographic Information Science (GIS), remote sensing, spatial modeling, and/or environmental informatics visualization— to understand and address environmental challenges across human and natural systems. We seek candidates who are committed to exemplifying the teacher-scholar model and whose scholarship engages issues such as environmental change, sustainability, ecosystem resilience, climate adaptation, environmental justice, water or land systems, urban or regional sustainability, socioecological systems, conservation science, or other environmental problems at local to global scales. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to our growing programs in Environmental Science, Environmental Studies, Geography, and GIS while helping students develop practical and analytical skills applicable to contemporary environmental problem-solving. The individual will also show interest in Common Good Community-Engaged courses to integrate community engagement in the curriculum. The ideal candidate will be able to teach introductory and advanced GIS and geospatial analysis courses, along with courses serving broader environmental curricula. Possible teaching responsibilities may include introductory/advanced GIS, remote sensing, environmental problem-solving, environmental science, environmental studies, sustainability, and upper-level courses in the candidate’s area of expertise. The standard teaching load is six, four-credit courses per year; faculty with active research labs at St. Thomas currently receive a research reallocation of one course per semester to mentor undergraduate student researchers. In addition to teaching and advising, the successful candidate will be expected to: maintain an active research agenda involving undergraduate collaborators and incorporating inclusive research practices; contribute to interdisciplinary initiatives across the department and university; participate in service to the department, university, and broader community, including cultivating and maintaining external partnerships; and support the University’s mission of educating students to think critically, act wisely, and advance the common good. About the Department of Earth, Environment, and Society The Department of Earth, Environment, and Society is an interdisciplinary department with programs in Environmental Science, Environmental Studies, Geography, GIS, Geology, and Sustainability. Faculty and students (approximately 80 majors) are located in O'Shaughnessy Science Hall and the new Schoenecker Center, the University’s interdisciplinary STEAM facility designed to support collaborative teaching and research across the sciences and humanities. The Department has excellent research and teaching laboratories, including new analytical instruments and a new GIS computer classroom. Learn more about the Department of Earth, Environment, and Society at the following website: Earth, Environment, and Society . About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. Learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. The successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website. Salary & Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits Salary range for this position is $68,000 - $74,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Minimum Qualifications A PhD (U.S. or Foreign Equivalent) in Environmental Science, Environmental Studies, Geography, Geology, Sustainability, Natural Resources or related field, or alternatively, ABD candidates nearing completion will also be considered, with an initial appointment made at the rank of instructor. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/. The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: A curriculum vitae (CV) A 2-page statement of teaching philosophy that includes a discussion of courses the applicant would be interested in teaching in our current curriculum, courses that enhance our current offerings, and the potential for inclusive classroom practices to support students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests A 2-page statement of short- and long-term research goals that includes a discussion of how the applicant will involve and mentor undergraduate collaborators in their research, establishing a vibrant, high-quality and inclusive research program at a well-equipped undergraduate institution that has the potential to be competitive for external funding Two pieces of evidence demonstrating teaching effectiveness, which may include student reports on instruction, peer evaluations, and/or other types of teaching assessment Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that describes the applicant’s interest in the position, discusses opportunities for efforts to work with community partners, engage in applied projects, and broaden participation in environmental and sustainability work, and relates the applicant’s professional qualifications to the expectations of the position Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation Questions regarding the position should be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Susan Caplow (scaplow@stthomas.edu ). Review of applications will begin on December 1st, 2026. To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. This position will remain open until filled. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
