    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9493/tenure-track%2c-assistant-professor-of-art-history-and-visual-culture/job": {
      "title": "Tenure Track, Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture",
      "description": "Tenure Track, Assistant Professor of Art History and Visual Culture Posted Date 3 months ago (7/24/2026 5:10 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Art History and Visual Culture in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas (St. Paul, MN) invites applications for a full-time, tenure-track position in the History of Art and Visual Culture of the Mediterranean Crossroads at the rank of assistant professor to begin in the fall semester of 2027. The position requires specialization in topics involving the Mediterranean world before 1700, considering the region as a meeting point for Greek, Roman, Byzantine, Islamic, Jewish, Christian, and North African civilizations. With this position we seek to expand the department’s curriculum to examine the Mediterranean as a hub of the exchange of cultural and scientific knowledge and visual dialogue. The successful candidate will teach an introductory undergraduate art history course and upper-level undergraduate art history courses focusing on the art history and visual culture of the Mediterranean Crossroads, serving students from across all disciplines at the university. The successful candidate will also develop graduate courses that serve students in our Master of Arts in Art History and Visual Culture and Graduate Certificate in Museum Studies programs. A commitment to innovative teaching and mentoring is required. The standard teaching load is six, four-credit in-person courses per year. Online and blended courses may also be offered based on need and in consultation with the department chair. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active scholarly agenda. We encourage applications from scholars who are able to enhance our curriculum with expertise in ancient, medieval, and/or early modern studies and with the ability to situate Christian visual culture within its broader intercultural contexts. Experience and/or interest in museum studies is welcome. In addition to teaching and scholarship, faculty are also expected to advise undergraduate and graduate students and participate actively in departmental, college, and university service. About the Department of Art History and Visual Culture The Department of Art History and Visual Culture offers an undergraduate major and minor in Art History and Visual Culture and an undergraduate minor in Museum Studies. We also offer a Master of Arts in Art History and Visual Culture and a Graduate Certificate in Museum Studies. Please see the Department of Art History and Visual Culture website for more information about our curriculum and program offerings. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. Learn more about life as a faculty member at the Join the Faculty at University of St. Thomas website. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity, and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristic. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range for this position is $65,000–$70,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a salary range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Applicants must have a Ph.D. (U.S. or foreign equivalent degree) in Art History, Archaeology, or a closely related field, or alternatively, ABD candidates nearing completion of the Ph.D. will be considered, with an initial appointment to be made at the rank of Instructor. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps , completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: Curriculum Vitae (CV) that includes relevant teaching experience in pre-1700 Mediterranean civilizations and imagery in cultural context A sample course syllabus for which you were a teaching assistant or instructor of record demonstrating approach to teaching in the specified subject area A writing sample (publication, dissertation chapter, etc.) demonstrating research agenda Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job-specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Submit a cover letter that addresses the following: your interest in the position, ability to teach courses in pre-1700 Mediterranean art and other courses that enhance our current offerings, your commitment to excellence in teaching and mentorship, scholarly agenda relative to the position, and your approaches to engagement with the university’s mission and convictions Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self-identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without your permission Candidates who advance to the finalist phase will be asked to have their three references submit letters of recommendation. No other documents will be considered during the initial screening of the application. Review of application materials will begin on October 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Please contact the search chair, Dr. Craig Eliason (cdeliason@stthomas.edu) if you have any questions. Finalists invited to campus will be expected to give a classroom lecture and a research presentation to the faculty. Established in 1885, the University of St. Thomas is located in the major metropolitan area of Minneapolis-St. Paul, and is Minnesota’s largest private university. Its 10,000 students pursue degrees in a wide range of liberal arts, professional, and graduate programs. Official job posting is available at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9465/assistant-professor%2c-psychological-generalist/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Psychological Generalist",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Psychological Generalist Posted Date 3 months ago (7/20/2026 9:21 AM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas (St. Paul, MN) is seeking a candidate for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position as a Psychological Generalist beginning Fall 2027. We seek a collaborative teacher-scholar who is passionate about liberal arts undergraduate education, committed to inclusive and engaging pedagogy, and eager to mentor students in research and applied inquiry. The department especially welcomes candidates who are committed to inclusive excellence and who have experience engaging students from diverse backgrounds through innovative and evidence-based teaching practices. Teaching responsibilities include: Teaching 5-6 courses, depending on lab courses, per academic year, delivered with flexible delivery including in-person, online, and mixed Research Methods in Psychology or History of Psychology in Social Context Other courses in our curriculum or new courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. Please see our Psychology Classes and Descriptions website for more information on our current curriculum. In addition to teaching, faculty responsibilities include: Student advising and research mentoring, maintaining an active scholarship agenda, providing service to the department and/or university, working with department colleagues to continually strengthen and advance the program. About the Department of Psychology The Psychology department seeks to attract, retain, and develop faculty from all backgrounds to enhance the diversity of our learning community and to offer students richly varied perspectives. St. Thomas also offers a robust faculty development/advancement program that provides support, including pre- and post-tenure grants, for teaching and research at all stages of a faculty member’s career. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. To learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas, please see our Join the Faculty at St. Thomas website. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristics. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website. SALARY AND BENEFITS The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range is for this position is $68,000-$74,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS Qualifications A doctoral degree (U.S. or foreign equivalent) in Psychology or related field, or alternatively, ABD candidates nearing completion will also be considered, with an initial appointment made at the rank of instructor. Applicants must be able to demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and show strong potential for productive research/scholarly activities. For full consideration, materials should be submitted online at www.stthomas.edu/jobs . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: A curriculum vitae (CV) Two pieces of evidence demonstrating teaching effectiveness, which may include student reports on instruction, peer evaluations, and/or other types of teaching assessment. For those with less experience, provide a statement of thoughtful approach for successful teaching in psychology courses for those with less experience, A written statement of teaching philosophy and research interests that includes a discussion of ability to teach Research Methods in Psychology or History of Psychology in Social Context, interest in teaching other courses that enhance our current offerings, the potential for inclusive classroom practices to support students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests, establishing a vibrant, high-quality and inclusive research program at a well-equipped undergraduate institution that has the potential to be competitive for external funding, and involvement and mentoring undergraduate collaborators in their research Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that describes the applicant’s interest in the position, relates the applicant’s professional qualifications to the expectations of the position, and describes commitment to the mission and convictions of the University Complete the required EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation Questions regarding the position should be sent to the department chair, Dr. Sarah Hankerson at sjhank@stthomas.edu . Application review begins Friday, September 18, 2026 . To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. The position will remain open until filled. In light of its commitment to create and maintain a safe learning and working environment, employment with the University of St. Thomas requires consent to and successful completion of a background screening. The University of St. Thomas, Minnesota Human Resources Department advertises the official job listing on its website at www.stthomas.edu/jobs. Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
    },
    "https://facultyemployment-stthomas.icims.com/jobs/9474/assistant-professor%2c-social-psychology/job": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Social Psychology",
      "description": "Assistant Professor, Social Psychology Posted Date 3 months ago (7/20/2026 12:05 PM) Campus St. Paul Employment Type Tenure Track Faculty FLSA Status Exempt/Salaried OVERVIEW The Department of Psychology in the College of Arts and Sciences at the University of St. Thomas invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Social Psychology beginning Fall 2027. We seek a collaborative teacher-scholar who is passionate about a liberal arts undergraduate education, committed to inclusive and engaging pedagogy, and eager to mentor students in research and applied inquiry. We especially welcome candidates who are enthusiastic about mentoring undergraduate students from diverse backgrounds in research, professional development, and have interest in offering Common Good Community-Engaged courses. Applicants should include a thoughtful plan on how they would incorporate students in their research programs. Teaching responsibilities include: Teaching 5-6 courses, depending on lab courses, per academic year, delivered with flexible delivery including in-person, online, and mixed Teaching expectations include Social Psychology and an upper-level lab class for Social Psychology Other courses in our curriculum or new courses aligned with the candidate’s expertise and departmental needs. Please see our Psychology Classes and Descriptions website for more information on our current curriculum. In addition to teaching, faculty responsibilities include: Student advising and research mentoring, Maintaining an active scholarship agenda, Working with department colleagues to continually strengthen and advance the program. About the Department of Psychology The Psychology department values a learning community enriched by varied perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds, and seeks colleagues who are committed to fostering inclusive and engaging learning environments. We are a collegial department that values collaboration, innovative teaching, and close faculty-student relationships. The department actively supports faculty scholarship through student collaboration, internal grants, conference funding, and pre- and post-tenure faculty development opportunities. About the University of St. Thomas The University of St. Thomas is a dynamic, mission-driven, urban university. Located in a residential neighborhood with easy access to two downtown metropolitan centers, we are Minnesota’s largest private university with eight schools and colleges, 150+ undergraduate majors/minors, and 55 graduate programs. To learn more about life as a faculty member at St. Thomas, please see our Join the Faculty at St. Thomas website. The University of St. Thomas embraces belonging and equal opportunity for all. Our convictions of dignity, diversity and personal attention call us to embody and champion an inclusive environment. The University is an Equal Employment Opportunity Employer (EEO). All qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, age, status as a protected veteran, or other protected characteristics. This commitment to inclusion and equal opportunity is consistent with our mission: Inspired by Catholic intellectual tradition, the University of St. Thomas educates students to be morally responsible leaders who think critically, act wisely, and work skillfully to advance the common good. A successful candidate will possess a commitment to the ideals of this mission. Learn more about the University of St. Thomas mission and convictions at the Office for Mission website. Salary and Benefits The University of St. Thomas offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits program, which includes: Tuition remission benefits for employees, spouses, and dependents upon eligibility A generous employer retirement contribution upon eligibility Medical, dental, and vision options Employer-paid disability, life, and AD&D benefits Paid parental leave benefits The salary range for this position is $68,000-$74,000. The University of St. Thomas has provided a compensation range that represents its good faith estimate of what the University may pay for the position at the time of posting. The specific salary offered will be determined based on factors such as the qualifications of the selected candidate, departmental budget availability, internal salary equity considerations, and available market information. QUALIFICATIONS A doctoral degree (U.S. or foreign equivalent) in Social Psychology or related field, or alternatively, ABD nearing completion will be considered, with appointment at the rank of instructor. Applicants must be able to demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching and show strong potential for productive research/scholarly activities. How to Apply To be considered for this opportunity, all applicants must apply online at https://www.stthomas.edu/jobs/ . The application process consists of several required steps, completed in the order outlined below. Step 1. You will first be asked to create an applicant profile. As part of creating your profile, you must upload the following materials: A curriculum vitae (CV) Two pieces of evidence demonstrating teaching effectiveness in social psychology-related courses, which may include student reports on instruction, peer evaluations, and/or other types of teaching assessment. For those with less experience, provide a statement of thoughtful approach for successful teaching in social psychology, A written statement of teaching philosophy and research interests that includes a discussion of ability to teach courses in the area of Social Psychology, interest in teaching other courses that enhance our current offerings, the potential for inclusive classroom practices to support students from varied backgrounds and with varied interests, establishing a vibrant, high-quality and inclusive research program at a well-equipped undergraduate institution that has the potential to be competitive for external funding, and involvement and mentoring undergraduate collaborators in their research Please upload only the documents specifically requested in the job posting at this stage. Do not upload any additional materials into other available fields. Candidates selected for further review may be contacted later to submit additional materials. Step 2. After submitting your applicant profile, you will be directed to the job‑specific application. During this step, you will be asked to: Copy and paste a job‑specific letter of application (cover letter) that describes the applicant’s interest in the position, relates the applicant’s professional qualifications to the expectations of the position, and describes commitment to the mission and convictions of the University Complete the voluntary EEO, Veteran, and Disability self‑identification forms Step 3. As part of the application process, you will be asked to provide contact information for three professional references who can speak directly to your teaching experience and qualifications. References will not be contacted without candidate permission Candidates who advance in the search will be asked to submit three letters of recommendation Questions regarding the position should be sent to the search committee chair, Dr. Annie Pezalla at peza1179@stthomas.edu. Application review begins Friday, September 18, 2026 . To be guaranteed full consideration, completed applications should be submitted by that date. The position will remain open until filled. In light of its commitment to create and maintain a safe learning and working environment, employment with the University of St. Thomas requires consent to and successful completion of a background screening. The University of St. Thomas, Minnesota Human Resources Department advertises the official job listing on its website at www.stthomas.edu/jobs . The University of St. Thomas is an Equal Opportunity Employer Options Apply for this job Apply Share Sorry the Share function is not working properly at this moment. Please refresh the page and try again later. Share on your newsfeed Want to get notified about new employment opportunities? To receive notifications regarding new job opportunities that fit your selected career interests Connect with us. Application FAQs Software Powered by ICIMS www.icims.com"
